<m>\left\langle x'(2), y'(2)\right\rangle</m>
</label>
<label at="q1a-plab" anchor="(1.55,2.30)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>\text{ant's path}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A portion of the x y plane between x equals zero point five five and two point four five. Nine data points are marked at the corners of a grid with x and y each equal to one, one point five and two, each labelled with its temperature. An orange curve, the ant's path, rises steeply through the point one comma one, and a dark red velocity arrow at that point points up and slightly to the left.">
  <annotation ref="q1a-path" text="The ant's path, given parametrically by x equals one minus one eighth of the natural log of two t minus three, and y equals two to the t over four."/>
  <annotation ref="q1a-vel" text="The velocity vector of the ant at t equals two, with components negative one quarter and natural log of two."/>
  <annotation ref="q1a-p11" text="The point one comma one, where the temperature is seventy degrees."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<!-- ===== From the old worksheet "Exercises for The Chain Rule" ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="rev-tp-4">
<statement>
<p>
Compute the partial derivatives of <m>f</m> with respect to
<m>x, y</m> for
<md>
f(s,t) = s^2 - t^2, \qquad
s = \frac{x+y}{2}, \qquad
t = \frac{x-y}{2},
</md>
using both the chain rule and by plugging in <m>s</m> and <m>t</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="8" due="2026-11-13"
<solution>
<p>
Using the chain rule:
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
\amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial s}\frac{\partial s}{\partial x}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}\frac{\partial t}{\partial x}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 2s\cdot\frac12 - 2t\cdot\frac12 = s - t = y</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
\amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial s}\frac{\partial s}{\partial y}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial t}\frac{\partial t}{\partial y}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 2s\cdot\frac12 + 2t\cdot\frac12 = s + t = x</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Plugging in, we see
<md>
f(s(x,y), t(x,y))
= \frac14\left((x+y)^2 - (x-y)^2\right) = xy,
</md>
and easily obtain the same partial derivatives.
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-tp-5">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider <m>f(x,y) = \left(\cos x + (1+x)\tan y\right)e^{x^2-1+y}</m>.
Then <m>\dfrac{d}{dt}f(t^2, t^3) = 0</m> at <m>t = 0</m>.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="8" due="2026-11-13"
<answer>
<p>True.</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
Writing <m>x(t) = t^2</m> and <m>y(t) = t^3</m>, we
see that
<md>
\frac{dx}{dt} = 2t = 0
\quad\text{and}\quad
\frac{dy}{dt} = 3t^2 = 0
\quad\text{at } t = 0.
</md>
Now we see from the chain rule that
<md>
\frac{d}{dt}f(x(t), y(t))
= \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{dx}{dt}
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{dy}{dt}
= 0 \quad\text{at } t = 0.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-cr-1">
<title>Homogeneous functions</title>
<statement>
<p>
A function <m>f</m> is called <term>homogeneous of degree
<m>n</m></term> if it satisfies the equation
<md xml:id="eq-rm2-homogeneous" number="yes">
f(tx, ty) = t^n f(x,y)
</md>
for all <m>t</m>, where <m>n</m> is a positive integer and <m>f</m>
has continuous second order derivatives.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Verify that <m>f(x,y) = x^2y + 2xy^2 + 5y^3</m> is homogeneous of
degree 3.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Show that if <m>f</m> is homogeneous of degree <m>n</m> then
<md>
x\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial x} + y\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
= n f(x,y).
</md>
(Hint: use the Chain Rule to differentiate <m>f(tx,ty)</m> with
respect to <m>t</m>.)
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
If <m>f</m> is homogeneous of degree <m>n</m>, show that
<m>f_x(tx, ty) = t^{n-1} f_x(x,y)</m> for <m>t \gt 0</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="8" due="2026-11-13"
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>f(tx, ty) \amp = t^2x^2\,ty + 2tx\,t^2y^2 + 5t^3y^3</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = t^3\left(x^2y + 2xy^2 + 5y^3\right) = t^3 f(x,y).</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Using the chain rule we obtain
<md>
<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}f(tx,ty)
\amp = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(tx,ty)\,\frac{d}{dt}tx
+ \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(tx,ty)\,\frac{d}{dt}ty</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-rm2-hom-chain" number="yes">\amp = x\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(tx,ty)
+ y\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}(tx,ty).</mrow>
</md>
On the other hand, the homogeneity condition
<xref ref="eq-rm2-homogeneous"/> shows that
<md xml:id="eq-rm2-hom-power" number="yes">
\frac{d}{dt}f(tx,ty) = \frac{d}{dt}\,t^n f(x,y)
= n t^{n-1} f(x,y).
</md>
Now we can equate the right hand sides of equations
<xref ref="eq-rm2-hom-chain"/> and <xref ref="eq-rm2-hom-power"/> and
set <m>t = 1</m> to obtain the desired equation.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
We take the partial <m>u</m>-derivative of both sides of the equation
<m>f(tu, tv) = t^n f(u,v)</m> (this is just
<xref ref="eq-rm2-homogeneous"/> plugging in <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>
instead of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>):
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial}{\partial u}\,t^n f(u,v)
\amp = t^n f_x(u,v)</mrow>
<mrow>\frac{\partial}{\partial u}\,f(tu, tv)
\amp = t\,f_x(tu, tv)</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>t^{n-1} f_x(u,v) = f_x(tu, tv)</m> after canceling one
<m>t</m>, and plugging in <m>u = x</m>, <m>v = y</m> shows the desired
equation.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-1">
<statement>
<p>
Let
<md>
w = 5xy + 2yz - 3xz, \qquad
x = st, \qquad y = e^{st}, \qquad z = t^2.
</md>
Compute <m>\dfrac{\partial w}{\partial s}(-4,-4)</m> and
<m>\dfrac{\partial w}{\partial t}(-4,-4)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="8" due="2026-11-13"
<solution>
<p>
The dependency tree is shown in <xref ref="fig-rw21-tree"/>. We first
compute
<md>
\frac{\partial w}{\partial x} = 5y - 3z, \qquad
\frac{\partial w}{\partial y} = 5x + 2z, \qquad
\frac{\partial w}{\partial z} = 2y - 3x,
</md>
and at <m>(s,t) = (-4,-4)</m> we have
<md>
x(-4,-4) = 16, \qquad
y(-4,-4) = e^{16}, \qquad
z(-4,-4) = 16.
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-rw21-tree">
<caption>
The tree diagram for <m>w = w(x,y,z)</m> with
<m>x = x(s,t)</m>, <m>y = y(s,t)</m>, and <m>z = z(t)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="42%">
<description>
<p>
A tree diagram with w at the top. Three branches lead down from w to
x, y, and z. From x, two branches lead to s and t; from y, two
branches lead to s and t; from z, a single branch leads to t.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
  \node (w) at (0,2.4) {$w$};
  \node (x) at (-1.8,1.2) {$x$};
  \node (y) at (0,1.2) {$y$};
  \node (z) at (1.8,1.2) {$z$};
  \node (s1) at (-2.4,0) {$s$};
  \node (t1) at (-1.2,0) {$t$};
  \node (s2) at (-0.6,0) {$s$};
  \node (t2) at (0.6,0) {$t$};
  \node (t3) at (1.8,0) {$t$};
  \draw[mblue, thick] (w) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (x);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (w) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (y);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (w) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (z);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (x) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (s1);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (x) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (t1);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (y) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (s2);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (y) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (t2);
  \draw[mblue, thick] (z) ]]>&#45;&#45;<![CDATA[ (t3);
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
By the chain rule, since
<m>\dfrac{\partial z}{\partial s} = 0</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial w}{\partial s}
\amp = \frac{\partial w}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial s}
+ \frac{\partial w}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial s}
+ \frac{\partial w}{\partial z}\frac{\partial z}{\partial s}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (5y - 3z)(t) + (5x + 2z)\left(t e^{st}\right)
+ (2y - 3x)(0)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left(5e^{16} - 48\right)(-4)
+ (80 + 32)\left(-4e^{16}\right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -20e^{16} + 192 - 448e^{16}
= -468e^{16} + 192.</mrow>
</md>
Similarly,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial w}{\partial t}
\amp = \frac{\partial w}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial t}
+ \frac{\partial w}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial t}
+ \frac{\partial w}{\partial z}\frac{\partial z}{\partial t}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (5y - 3z)(s) + (5x + 2z)\left(s e^{st}\right)
+ (2y - 3x)(2t)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left(5e^{16} - 48\right)(-4)
+ (112)\left(-4e^{16}\right)
+ \left(2e^{16} - 48\right)(-8)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -20e^{16} + 192 - 448e^{16} - 16e^{16} + 384
= -484e^{16} + 576.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
